    "explanation": "To the rider and to the driver. Nothing is paid and nothing is refunded today: this case goes to a human reviewer, and the 14.05 SGD fare (EV-FARE-05009) is held rather than collected or returned. Here is why, in plain language.\n\nWhat the record does settle. The 23 Aug 2026 trip ran Causeway Point to Hillion Mall. The platform's own recorded distances on EV-TRIP-05009 are 10.0 km actual against 8.5 km recommended, so under POL-RD-22 the deviation is 1.50 km - over the 1.0 km tolerance and under POL-RD-25's 2 km ceiling for an AI ruling. The driver did notify the rider in-app before the deviation: MSG-05009-003 at 15:49:39 ('BKE got accident near Dairy Farm, I take Woodlands Road ok?') against the route_deviation_detected event at 15:51:59, and the rider read it and answered 'ok can' at 15:49:59 (EV-CHAT-05009). The notification limb of POL-RD-21 is met, and at the base rate on EV-FARE-05009 (0.72 SGD/km, POL-RD-24) the excess-distance figure would be 1.50 x 0.72 = 1.08 SGD. Those points are the rider's claim 1 and the driver's claims 2 and 3, and they are accepted so far as they go.\n\nWhat the record does not settle, and why the AI cannot rule. POL-RD-24's matrix turns on whether the reroute was traffic-justified, and the only artifact that could carry that fact - the LTA traffic advisory for the recommended BKE segment over the trip window, EV-ADVIS-05009-01 - is MISSING: weight_multiplier 0.0, issue code MISSING_ARTIFACT, and under POL-GEN-02 a missing artifact carries no fact. The pre-pass records that the platform's own request to the LTA feed timed out and was not retried, and that the record is 'silent on traffic on the recommended route; this is not a finding of no traffic.' POL-GEN-26 names 'whether traffic justified the reroute' among the facts that send a case to a human reviewer when the evidence that would carry it is missing or impaired, and the clause requires no outcome to be inferred from that absence.\n\nSo both of the two competing readings fail. The rider's claim that the case is a straight row (d) refund of 1.08 SGD is rejected: POL-RD-23 does provide that a driver's assertion of traffic with no supporting data does not justify the reroute, and the driver's bare assertion (in chat and in his statement, 'Got accident, I saw the jam on the map') therefore cannot win the traffic-justified limb - but it does not establish the opposite either, and with the carrying artifact absent the traffic limb is undecided, supporting neither a refund that needs justification to fail nor a no-refund that needs it to hold. The driver's claim that POL-GEN-02(2) caps confidence at the missing artifact's 0.0 answers itself: a missing artifact carries no fact, so it fixes no cap; but nor does its absence prove the reroute unjustified. The driver's POL-RD-21 point (informed and agreed) is accepted as a fact, but it only decides between refund and no-refund once traffic justification is known - it is an element of rows (a) and (b) of POL-RD-24 and does not bear on row (d).\n\nOpen question for the reviewer: was the recommended BKE segment congested, incident-affected or closed between 15:46 and 16:04 on 23 Aug 2026? If it was, the driver informed the rider and the rider agreed, so no refund is due (row (a)). If it was not, the excess-distance fare of 1.08 SGD is refundable under row (d). The 14.05 SGD fare is held until that is answered. No referral issues: a route deviation has no referral clause in this policy version, and POL-RD-25's 0.80 confidence bar for an AI ruling is not met on a pivotal fact this record cannot carry."
